Chapter 7B research completed for Andrés Manuel López Obrador, Mexico, 2022. No score assigned and no other chapter researched.

The attempt-directory manifest could not be updated because the filesystem sandbox failed with `bwrap: loopback: Failed RTM_NEWADDR`. A cumulative manifest is therefore included inline below.

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Falso o engañoso el 64% del discurso de AMLO en mañaneras","publisher":"Verificado","publication_date":"2022-02-21","url":"https://verificado.com.mx/falso-o-enganoso-el-64-del-discurso-de-amlo-en-mananeras-de-ultima-semana/","claim":"Verificado assessed 25 factual statements made during 14–18 February 2022: 10 false, 6 misleading, and 9 true; it also found that claims concerning government corruption, journalist repression, and relations involving the president’s son did not match the available evidence.","locator":"Sections on 14–18 February 2022 findings, paragraphs 131–139","provisional_id":"WEB-7B-001","canonical_fact_key":"https://verificado.com.mx/falso-o-enganoso-el-64-del-discurso-de-amlo-en-mananeras-de-ultima-semana/|131-139|February 2022 factual-claim assessment","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["7B"],"methodology_ids":["7B.1","7B.2","7B.3","7B.4"],"source_type":"fact_check","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Named fact-checking organization identified the sample, method, dates, and underlying official/comparative sources.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers presidential statements in February 2022.","ruler_attribution":"The evidence concerns statements made personally by López Obrador in official morning conferences.","contrary_evidence":["The article records nine statements as true, so it does not establish universal falsity.","Some findings concern administration-wide claims rather than provable personal deception."],"lenses":["7B.1","7B.2","7B.3","7B.4"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Falso y engañoso el discurso de 4° Informe de Gobierno","publisher":"Verificado","publication_date":"2022-09-05","url":"https://verificado.com.mx/falso-y-enganoso-discurso-4-informe-de-gobierno/","claim":"Verificado examined 30 factual messages in López Obrador’s 1 September 2022 Fourth Government Report and classified 9 as true, 8 as misleading, and 13 as false; it also characterized the speech as emphasizing future promises and projects more than concrete results.","locator":"Sections beginning “A 44 meses…” and “Promesas más que resultados,” paragraphs 131–141","provisional_id":"WEB-7B-002","canonical_fact_key":"https://verificado.com.mx/falso-y-enganoso-discurso-4-informe-de-gobierno/|131-141|Fourth Report factual assessment","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["7B"],"methodology_ids":["7B.1","7B.2","7B.8"],"source_type":"fact_check","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"The source states its sample size, methodology, classification results, and publication date.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly evaluates a major 2022 presidential report.","ruler_attribution":"The assessed claims were made by López Obrador in his own government report.","contrary_evidence":["Nine of thirty claims were classified true.","Fact-checking accuracy does not alone establish whether every error was deliberate."],"lenses":["7B.1","7B.2","7B.8"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Nepotismo en el gobierno federal | Anuario de la corrupción 2022","publisher":"Mexicanos Contra la Corrupción y la Impunidad","publication_date":"2023-01-01","url":"https://contralacorrupcion.mx/anuario-de-la-corrupcion-2022/nepotismo-en-el-gobierno-federal/","claim":"The 2022 corruption yearbook documented family relationships among federal officials and reported that media investigations had identified personal relationships between government contractors, officials, and López Obrador’s sons; it stated that the president had not introduced legislation regulating possible conflicts of interest involving himself and his family.","locator":"Nepotismo en el gobierno federal section, paragraph 13","provisional_id":"WEB-7B-003","canonical_fact_key":"https://contralacorrupcion.mx/anuario-de-la-corrupcion-2022/nepotismo-en-el-gobierno-federal/|13|family and conflict-of-interest reporting","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["7B"],"methodology_ids":["7B.3","7B.4","7B.9"],"source_type":"civil_society_investigation","source_confidence":"medium","source_confidence_reason":"Civil-society compilation with named positions and reported investigations, but not a judicial finding of personal wrongdoing.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Explicitly reports developments during 2022.","ruler_attribution":"The evidence concerns alleged relationships involving the president’s family and federal administration; it does not prove López Obrador personally profited.","contrary_evidence":["The source does not establish that López Obrador directed contracts or personally received benefits.","Some listed relationships concern ordinary bureaucratic family employment rather than presidential appointments."],"lenses":["7B.3","7B.4","7B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"La SFP exonera a AMLO, su hijo y Daniel Chávez de conflicto de interés","publisher":"Expansión Política","publication_date":"2022-05-06","url":"https://politica.expansion.mx/mexico/2022/05/06/sfp-libera-a-amlo-su-hijo-y-daniel-chavez","claim":"The Secretaría de la Función Pública opened an investigation on 16 February 2022 into the Casa Gris allegations and reported that it found no competent and conclusive evidence of an administrative violation attributable to López Obrador, his son, Daniel Chávez, or related parties.","locator":"Paragraphs 78 and 87–90","provisional_id":"WEB-7B-004","canonical_fact_key":"https://politica.expansion.mx/mexico/2022/05/06/sfp-libera-a-amlo-su-hijo-y-daniel-chavez|87-90|SFP investigation outcome","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["7B"],"methodology_ids":["7B.3","7B.4","7B.6"],"source_type":"official-investigation_reported_by_press","source_confidence":"medium","source_confidence_reason":"Reports the government investigation and its stated conclusion, while not independently reproducing the complete case file.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Investigation and disposition occurred in February–May 2022.","ruler_attribution":"The finding is relevant to the president’s willingness to permit a formal investigation, but does not independently validate the investigation’s completeness.","contrary_evidence":["Civil-society and opposition sources disputed the adequacy of the exoneration.","The investigation found no administrative violation, not that no appearance of conflict existed."],"lenses":["7B.3","7B.4","7B.6"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"22.08.22 Versión estenográfica de la conferencia matutina","publisher":"Presidencia de la República","publication_date":"2022-08-22","url":"https://amlo.presidente.gob.mx/22-08-22-version-estenografica-de-la-conferencia-de-prensa-matutina-del-presidente-andres-manuel-lopez-obrador/","claim":"Asked about nepotism, López Obrador called cronyism, influence-peddling, and nepotism “lacras” of old politics that should be eliminated, while acknowledging that change would take time.","locator":"Transcript lines 170–171","provisional_id":"WEB-7B-005","canonical_fact_key":"https://amlo.presidente.gob.mx/22-08-22-version-estenografica-de-la-conferencia-de-prensa-matutina-del-presidente-andres-manuel-lopez-obrador/|170-171|presidential anti-nepotism statement","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["7B"],"methodology_ids":["7B.5","7B.9","7B.10"],"source_type":"official_transcript","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous official transcript of the president’s own answer.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly dated 22 August 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Direct statement by López Obrador.","contrary_evidence":["A stated ethical position does not demonstrate implementation.","The same answer acknowledges that the practices persisted."],"lenses":["7B.5","7B.9","7B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Sabotaje de periodistas “de élite” contra el gobierno, dice López Obrador","publisher":"La Jornada","publication_date":"2022-02-15","url":"https://www.jornada.com.mx/noticia/2022/02/15/politica/acusa-amlo-sabotaje-de-periodistas-de-elite-a-su-gobierno-2190","claim":"López Obrador requested investigations into journalist Carlos Loret de Mola’s income, assets, and family wealth, describing critical reporting as possible sabotage and contrasting journalism with defamation.","locator":"Presidential letter and quoted statements, paragraphs 81–93","provisional_id":"WEB-7B-006","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.jornada.com.mx/noticia/2022/02/15/politica/acusa-amlo-sabotaje-de-periodistas-de-elite-a-su-gobierno-2190|81-93|request to investigate critical journalist","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["7B"],"methodology_ids":["7B.6","7B.7","7B.10"],"source_type":"national_press_report","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Publishes the president’s contemporaneous letter and direct quotations.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly dated February 2022.","ruler_attribution":"The request and rhetoric are personally attributed to López Obrador.","contrary_evidence":["He framed the request as a transparency measure rather than retaliation.","The source does not establish that an investigation was actually conducted or that the journalist committed wrongdoing."],"lenses":["7B.6","7B.7","7B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Primer semestre de 2022: La impunidad y negación ante la violencia extrema contra la prensa persiste","publisher":"ARTICLE 19 México y Centroamérica","publication_date":"2022-08-18","url":"https://articulo19.org/informe-semestral-2022/","claim":"ARTICLE 19 recorded 331 attacks against journalists and media in January–June 2022, including 12 journalist killings, and identified state actors as responsible for 128 attacks; it described presidential-level stigmatization of critical journalists as producing a cascading effect.","locator":"Summary and paragraphs 56–74","provisional_id":"WEB-7B-007","canonical_fact_key":"https://articulo19.org/informe-semestral-2022/|56-74|press attacks and official stigmatization","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["7B"],"methodology_ids":["7B.7","7B.10"],"source_type":"press_freedom_monitor","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Specialist monitoring organization provides counts, comparison period, attribution categories, and methodology context.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Covers the first half of 2022.","ruler_attribution":"The report specifically identifies the federal executive’s rhetoric as part of the environment, while not attributing every attack personally to the president.","contrary_evidence":["Many attacks were attributed to state-level authorities or criminal actors.","The report documents environment and official conduct, not a direct presidential order to attack journalists."],"lenses":["7B.7","7B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Mexico: Address Persistent Violence Against Journalists","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2022-05-03","url":"https://www.hrw.org/news/2022/05/03/mexico-address-persistent-violence-against-journalists","claim":"Human Rights Watch reported eight journalists killed in Mexico by 3 May 2022, near-total impunity, and a pattern in which López Obrador used morning conferences to harass or intimidate critical journalists and display their names or personal information.","locator":"Paragraphs 606–625, especially 609–615","provisional_id":"WEB-7B-008","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.hrw.org/news/2022/05/03/mexico-address-persistent-violence-against-journalists|606-615|presidential harassment and impunity","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["7B"],"methodology_ids":["7B.6","7B.7","7B.10"],"source_type":"human_rights_report","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Established human-rights monitor cites official and specialist sources and distinguishes violence, investigation, and presidential conduct.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Published during and focused on 2022 conditions.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributes conference conduct to López Obrador; broader impunity is institutional and inherited.","contrary_evidence":["HRW’s figures cover national conditions involving criminal and subnational actors.","The source does not show that the president personally ordered specific attacks."],"lenses":["7B.6","7B.7","7B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Annual Report 2022, Mexico chapter","publisher":"Inter-American Commission on Human Rights / OAS","publication_date":"2023-03-06","url":"https://www.oas.org/en/iachr/docs/annual/2022/Chapters/14-IA2022_Cap_5_MX_EN.pdf","claim":"The IACHR Special Rapporteur reported attacks against journalists every 14 hours, widespread fear around reporting on corruption and organized crime, and repeated stigmatizing statements by public officials; it found the government’s “Who’s Who in Lies” section was used to stigmatize and disqualify press work.","locator":"Mexico chapter, paragraphs 391–392, PDF pages 92–93","provisional_id":"WEB-7B-009","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.oas.org/en/iachr/docs/annual/2022/Chapters/14-IA2022_Cap_5_MX_EN.pdf|P92-93 paragraphs 391-392|press stigmatization","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["7B"],"methodology_ids":["7B.7","7B.10"],"source_type":"intergovernmental_report","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Regional human-rights monitoring body synthesizing documented cases and civil-society evidence.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Annual report covers 2022 and cites 2022 incidents.","ruler_attribution":"Attributes the stigmatizing environment to public officials and the executive’s media initiative, not every individual attack to López Obrador.","contrary_evidence":["The IACHR recognizes that governments may legitimately respond to misinformation.","The report describes a broader political environment, not only presidential conduct."],"lenses":["7B.7","7B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"2022 Country Report on Human Rights Practices: Mexico","publisher":"U.S. Department of State","publication_date":"2023-03-20","url":"https://www.ecoi.net/en/document/2089118.html","claim":"The country report identified credible 2022 reports of serious government corruption, unlawful killings, forced disappearances, torture, restrictions on expression, and insufficient accountability for abuses.","locator":"Executive summary, paragraph 40","provisional_id":"WEB-7B-010","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.ecoi.net/en/document/2089118.html|40|2022 corruption and accountability findings","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["7B"],"methodology_ids":["7B.6","7B.7","7B.10"],"source_type":"government_human_rights_report","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Annual country report using a defined diplomatic human-rights reporting process and multiple cited sources.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Explicitly covers 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Findings concern the Mexican state and administration; they do not by themselves prove personal presidential participation.","contrary_evidence":["The report identifies credible allegations rather than adjudicated personal liability.","Many abuses involve military, police, prosecutors, or state-level actors."],"lenses":["7B.6","7B.7","7B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"En vigor, control operativo y administrativo de la Guardia Nacional a cargo de la Sedena","publisher":"Cámara de Diputados","publication_date":"2022-09-10","url":"https://comunicacionsocial.diputados.gob.mx/index.php/notilegis/en-vigor-control-operativo-y-administrativo-de-la-guardia-nacional-a-cargo-de-la-sedena","claim":"The September 2022 reform placed National Guard operational and administrative control with the Defense Ministry while formally retaining the Guard under the civilian security ministry; the official rationale cited criminal-violence pressures, human-rights safeguards, and separation of responsibilities.","locator":"Nota 3811, paragraphs 57–68 and 82–116","provisional_id":"WEB-7B-011","canonical_fact_key":"https://comunicacionsocial.diputados.gob.mx/index.php/notilegis/en-vigor-control-operativo-y-administrativo-de-la-guardia-nacional-a-cargo-de-la-sedena|57-68|National Guard transfer and official rationale","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["7B"],"methodology_ids":["7B.7","7B.8","7B.10"],"source_type":"legislative_record","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official legislative record states the enacted changes, rationale, safeguards, and presidential initiative provenance.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Enacted and effective in September 2022.","ruler_attribution":"The record states the reforms derived from an initiative presented on behalf of the president.","contrary_evidence":["The official text says the reform was intended to prevent abuse and preserve human-rights standards.","The measure passed through Congress rather than being imposed solely by executive order."],"lenses":["7B.7","7B.8","7B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Publica DOF reforma constitucional que amplía tiempo de participación de la Fuerza Armada","publisher":"Cámara de Diputados","publication_date":"2022-11-18","url":"https://comunicacionsocial.diputados.gob.mx/index.php/notilegis/publica-dof-reforma-constitucional-que-amplia-tiempo-de-participacion-de-la-fuerza-armada-en-tareas-de-seguridad-publica","claim":"A November 2022 constitutional reform extended presidential authority to deploy the permanent armed forces in public security for nine years, while requiring extraordinary, regulated, supervised, subordinated participation and periodic reporting to Congress.","locator":"Nota 4682, paragraphs 57–84","provisional_id":"WEB-7B-012","canonical_fact_key":"https://comunicacionsocial.diputados.gob.mx/index.php/notilegis/publica-dof-reforma-constitucional-que-amplia-tiempo-de-participacion-de-la-fuerza-armada-en-tareas-de-seguridad-publica|57-84|constitutional military-security extension","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["7B"],"methodology_ids":["7B.7","7B.8"],"source_type":"constitutional_legislative_record","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official account of the constitutional text and congressional approval process.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly enacted in November 2022.","ruler_attribution":"The reform expressly expands the president’s legal authority, but was constitutionally approved by Congress and state legislatures.","contrary_evidence":["The reform contains explicit reporting, supervision, and human-rights conditions.","Formal legal authority does not prove abusive exercise."],"lenses":["7B.7","7B.8"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Pío López Obrador: la Fiscalía desiste de acusar a un hermano de López Obrador","publisher":"EL PAÍS México","publication_date":"2022-10-25","url":"https://elpais.com/mexico/2022-10-25/la-fiscalia-desiste-de-acusar-a-un-hermano-de-lopez-obrador-por-las-grabaciones-en-las-que-recibe-dinero-para-campanas.html","claim":"The electoral prosecutor closed a two-year investigation into videos showing Pío López Obrador receiving approximately one million pesos connected to Morena campaign financing, concluding that no electoral crime would be prosecuted, subject to judicial confirmation.","locator":"Paragraphs 70–72","provisional_id":"WEB-7B-013","canonical_fact_key":"https://elpais.com/mexico/2022-10-25/la-fiscalia-desiste-de-acusar-a-un-hermano-de-lopez-obrador-por-las-grabaciones-en-las-que-recibe-dinero-para-campanas.html|70-72|FEDE closure of Pío López investigation","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["7B"],"methodology_ids":["7B.4","7B.6","7B.7","7B.9"],"source_type":"investigative_press_report","source_confidence":"medium","source_confidence_reason":"Reports a formal prosecutorial decision and identifies the underlying video evidence, but the president’s personal role is not established.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Decision occurred in October 2022 after a prolonged investigation.","ruler_attribution":"Relevant to accountability involving a close family member; no evidence here proves presidential intervention.","contrary_evidence":["The decision was made by an electoral prosecutor and required judicial confirmation.","The record does not show López Obrador directed closure or personally benefited."],"lenses":["7B.4","7B.6","7B.7","7B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Conflicto de intereses en el gobierno de AMLO","publisher":"Mexicanos Contra la Corrupción y la Impunidad","publication_date":"2024-01-01","url":"https://contralacorrupcion.mx/corrupcion-en-el-sexenio-de-amlo/conflicto-de-interes-en-el-gobierno-de-amlo/","claim":"MCCI summarized the January 2022 Casa Gris investigation: López Obrador’s son and partner rented a Houston residence owned by a senior Baker Hughes executive while Baker Hughes held Pemex contracts; the organization presented this as a potential conflict, while noting denials and the absence of a definitive finding in the cited episode.","locator":"Casa Gris section, paragraphs 29–46","provisional_id":"WEB-7B-014","canonical_fact_key":"https://contralacorrupcion.mx/corrupcion-en-el-sexenio-de-amlo/conflicto-de-interes-en-el-gobierno-de-amlo/|29-46|Casa Gris potential conflict","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["7B"],"methodology_ids":["7B.3","7B.4"],"source_type":"investigative_civil_society_report","source_confidence":"medium","source_confidence_reason":"Investigative organization identifies the property owner, family relationship, company, and public-contract connection, but the claim is not an adjudicated finding of presidential self-dealing.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"The underlying events and public controversy began in January–February 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Evidence concerns the president’s immediate family and possible access/conflict; it does not establish personal receipt of money by López Obrador.","contrary_evidence":["Baker Hughes and the president denied the conflict.","The SFP later reported no conclusive administrative violation."],"lenses":["7B.3","7B.4"]}

Cumulative manifest status: prior entries retained conceptually from the supplied index: AMLO22-R01, AMLO22-R02, AMLO22-R04, AMLO22-R05, AMLO22-R06, AMLO22-R07, AMLO22-R08, AMLO22-R09, AMLO22-R10, AMLO22-R11, AMLO22-R12; new entries: WEB-7B-001 through WEB-7B-014.

Research accounting:

- Documents discovered: approximately 40 plausible documents.
- Opened: 27 underlying pages, reports, transcripts, or PDFs.
- Newly accepted: 14 source-claim units.
- Reused evidence: 11 supplied records; not counted as newly discovered.
- Rejected: 6 items, mainly snippets, duplicated coverage, retrospective summaries, or insufficiently precise claims.
- Access-blocked: 5 pages/PDFs, including some government and legislative endpoints.
- Distinct organizations: at least 13.
- Source types: official transcripts, legislative records, constitutional/legal records, fact-checks, investigative journalism, civil-society reports, press-freedom monitoring, human-rights reports, and intergovernmental reporting.

Lens coverage:

- 7B.1: covered through fact-checks and presidential public claims; residual gap is intent behind inaccuracies.
- 7B.2: covered through repeated false/misleading claims and official doubling-down; no definitive evidence of private knowledge for every statement.
- 7B.3: covered through Casa Gris and SFP findings; no adjudicated proof of personal intervention.
- 7B.4: covered through Casa Gris, family-contract, and Pío López evidence; no finding that AMLO personally profited.
- 7B.5: covered only weakly through anti-nepotism statements and family-network reporting; direct evidence comparing appointment competence versus loyalty is a residual gap.
- 7B.6: covered through SFP and FEDE investigations, INAI-related accountability context, and documented impunity; independence and completeness remain contested.
- 7B.7: covered strongly through journalist harassment, stigmatization, military expansion, and accountability concerns.
- 7B.8: covered through the National Guard and armed-forces reforms; direct promise-by-promise documentation remains limited.
- 7B.9: covered through nepotism, family-network, and prosecutorial-accountability evidence; no comprehensive appointment/procurement dataset was available.
- 7B.10: covered strongly through the contrast between official anti-corruption/ethical rhetoric and documented misinformation, press stigmatization, impunity, and conflict-of-interest controversies.

Another broad chapter-specific wave would have limited material value now: all ten lenses have at least one defensible evidence unit, and the remaining gaps require narrowly targeted primary records—especially appointment files, procurement databases, and complete investigation dossiers—rather than more general web searching.
